## Summary

- Adds `createTypegpuAdapter()` in `packages/core/src/runtime/adapters/typegpu.ts`
- Wires it into the deterministic adapter array in `init.ts`
- Declares `window.__hfTypegpuTime` in `window.d.ts`
- 9 unit tests, all passing

## What it does

Enables fully seekable GPU-rendered compositions built with [TypeGPU](https://docs.swmansion.com/TypeGPU) or raw WebGPU. WebGPU pipelines have no introspectable global registry (unlike GSAP timelines or Lottie instances), so this adapter uses the same **push + poll** pattern as the Three.js adapter:

**Push** — dispatches an `"hf-seek"` CustomEvent on `window` so compositions can imperatively re-render at the seek position:
```js
window.addEventListener("hf-seek", (e) => render(e.detail.time));
```

**Poll** — sets `window.__hfTypegpuTime` so render loops can read the current time without registering an event listener:
```js
function rafLoop() {
  render(window.__hfTypegpuTime ?? 0);
  requestAnimationFrame(rafLoop);
}
```

## Usage in a composition

```html
<canvas id="gpu-canvas" width="1920" height="1080"></canvas>
<script type="module">
  const adapter = await navigator.gpu.requestAdapter();
  const device  = await adapter.requestDevice();
  // ... build your pipeline, buffers, bind groups ...

  function render(timeSeconds) {
    device.queue.writeBuffer(uniformBuf, 0, new Float32Array([timeSeconds]));
    // ... submit command encoder ...
  }

  // Seek — fired on every player scrub and rAF tick
  window.addEventListener("hf-seek", (e) => render(e.detail.time));

  // Initial frame
  render(window.__hfTypegpuTime ?? 0);
</script>
```

