## What this fixes

When Studio's element edit lands after another writer changed the file, undoing it could erase that writer's change if Studio's history claim arrived more than ten seconds after the edit.

Studio reads the file, then asks the server to patch one element. If an agent turn or an outside tool changed the file between Studio's read and the patch, Studio's claim says it overwrote its own earlier read, which is now stale. History fixes that by looking up the bytes the server's write actually replaced and splitting the other writer's change there. Those bytes lived only in the server's in-memory write record, which keeps them for ten seconds. A later claim found nothing, history gave the whole change to Studio, and undo wrote the stale read back over the other writer's change.

Now the project history keeps those bytes itself. Every server write hands the bytes it replaced to the open history. History notes which version replaced which, stores the bytes in its own on-disk store if it has never seen them, and cuts there when the claim comes, however late. The write record goes back to one job, recognising Studio's own saves when the file watcher reports them, and keeps its ten-second lifetime.

## Keeping the notes true

A note that outlives its moment can be worse than no note: if an editor later brings back bytes a server write once produced, an old note could send the cut to an even older save. So history drops notes as soon as they can no longer apply:

- when the change they belong to is committed, since a claim never walks a committed change;
- when history reads a file state that no server write produced (an editor or agent save) and no server write landed during that read, since no walk can pass that state;
- when history sees the file removed, since a removal ends the line of server writes (a file re-created with a note's exact bytes no longer inherits it);
- on every server write, keeping only the chain that leads back to the bytes it replaced, since a file's writes form one line and anything off it was already written over. A rolled-back server write's note goes this way at the next write.

Stored bytes stay protected from the history's size budget only while a note points at them. Once history is over its budget it now removes every stored blob nothing points at before it folds any entry, and it does so even when no entry can be folded (the oldest one pinned). Before, bytes were only removed as part of a fold, so a history stuck behind a pin kept its unreferenced bytes for good. If the history folder is removed while the project is open, storing replaced bytes recreates it, as every other history write already does.

Whether the replaced bytes still need storing is decided inside the history's queue, so a cleanup already under way cannot delete bytes a note just started pointing at.

Two races have no test, because the public history API cannot pause a sweep mid-read or a cleanup mid-delete: a server write landing while a sweep reads the same file (handled by the reading rule above), and one landing while the budget cleanup runs (handled by the queue).

## Visible change

None when nothing races. When something does, undoing a Studio edit restores the other writer's content, not Studio's stale read, whenever the claim arrives.

A claim only takes changes history has not yet committed, before and after this change. So the late claim this fixes is one that arrives while the other writer's change is still open: most often an agent turn, whose change stays open for the whole turn, or outside writes arriving less than two seconds apart, which keep their group open for up to 30 seconds. Once an outside change has committed on its own, a late claim finds nothing to take, on main and here alike.

## Tests

- `routes/history.test.ts`: two new cases run the real routes and claim 11 seconds after the patch, past the write record's lifetime, with the stale read Studio sends: one where the other write came from an agent turn still open, one from outside. Undo keeps the other write.
- `history/projectHistory.test.ts`:
  - a server write's note is forgotten once its change is committed, and once an editor writes over it, so a later claim does not cut at an older save;
  - after a rolled-back server write, a claim cuts at the editor's save, not at what the rolled-back write replaced;
  - a budget fold that runs before the claim keeps the bytes the claim will cut at;
  - a committed server write no longer holds its bytes against the budget;
  - past its budget, history removes bytes nothing points at even when the oldest entry is pinned;
  - a note is dropped when its file is removed, so a file re-created with the same bytes gets the client's word;
  - bytes a server write replaced are still stored after the history folder was removed while open.
- One existing test recorded a server write before its bytes reached disk, which no route does (every route records right after writing). It now follows that order and checks the same outcome.

Results: studio-server `tsc`, whole-tree `oxfmt --check` and `bun run lint`, fallow and the comment ratchet are clean. The whole studio-server suite passes (895 tests). On main, both 11-second cases fail. Each safeguard removed on its own fails its own test: commit-time forgetting, the budget protection, forgetting at an editor's state, the one-chain rule, forgetting on removal, freeing bytes when nothing can fold, and recreating the history folder. Moving the "already stored?" check back out of the queue fails no test, as noted above.

## What this fixes

Each project's undo history lives in its own folder under the history cache (`~/.cache/hyperframes/history` by default), and nothing ever removed one. Deleting a project left its history behind, blobs and all, so the cache only grew, by gigabytes a day on a machine that creates many short-lived projects.

Now opening a history also clears out the histories of projects that are gone, in the background, so an open never waits on it. Once a run finishes, a stamp in the history cache holds off the next one for a day, across all processes. A one-off CLI command (such as an agent's `hyperframes history` call) spends at most about one second on it and writes no stamp unless it finished, so a large backlog drains over a few commands and then settles into once a day. A long-running host (the Studio server, the Vite dev server) runs it to the end. A history is removed when all of these hold:

- the folder it was recorded for is gone, or now holds a different project;
- no running process holds it open;
- the project was under the system temp folder, where agent and test projects live, or the history has not been used for 14 days.

The check runs again under the history's own lock before anything is deleted, so an open that races the cleanup keeps its history. The history is first renamed out of the way in one step, so a cleanup interrupted mid-delete leaves a marked folder the next run finishes, never a half-deleted history. A history that cannot be checked (for example a permission error) is reported and skipped; the rest are still cleaned. Only folders named like a history are ever considered.

`pruneGoneProjectHistories` is exported with a dry run that reports what would go, with each project's folder and size, for a `clean` command to build on.

## What a user can lose

History follows a project when it is renamed or moved, but the new location is only learned when the project is opened again. So a project that is moved and then not opened for 14 days loses its undo history; one under the temp folder loses it at the next cleanup. A history whose project id file cannot be read is always kept.

A project on a drive or share that is not connected is not counted as gone. History notes which disk each project is on; when the project is missing, it looks at the nearest folder that still exists (the project folder itself, or one above it), and only if that is on the same disk does the project count as deleted. An unplugged external drive, a missing drive letter, an offline network share, or an empty mount point left where a drive was mounted leaves the history alone until the drive is back. Histories recorded before this change carry no disk note, and an empty mount point looks just like a deleted project's parent. For those, the project counts as deleted only when its own folder is still there without the project in it, or when it was under the temp folder; otherwise the history is kept until the project is opened again (which records its disk). So an older history of a project deleted outside the temp folder stays until then, erring on the side of keeping it.

## Tests

`history/pruneHistories.test.ts`:

- a deleted project's history is removed once no process holds it, and a live project's is kept;
- opening a history a day after the last cleanup removes a deleted project's history;
- a folder that now holds another project loses the old project's history;
- a project whose id file cannot be read keeps its history;
- a gone project whose disk is not connected keeps its history, as does an empty mount point, while a folder tree deleted from the same disk is cleaned;
- outside the temp folder a gone project's history is kept a day before the 14 days are up, and removed at 14 days;
- a history recorded without its disk is removed only when its folder is there without the project, or under the temp folder, and kept when its folder or parent is missing;
- a history whose project comes back while the cleanup takes its lock is kept;
- a dry run reports what would go, with folder and size, and removes nothing;
- an interrupted removal is finished, and a folder that is not a history is left alone;
- a history that cannot be checked is reported, and the others are still cleaned;
- a run whose time budget runs out (as a CLI command's can) writes no stamp, so the next open still cleans.

Results: studio-server and CLI `tsc`, whole-tree `oxfmt --check` and `bun run lint`, and fallow are clean. The whole studio-server suite passes (864 tests). On main the new test file fails (the cleanup does not exist). Each safeguard removed on its own fails a test: the daily trigger, the temp-folder rule, finishing interrupted removals, reporting one history's error without stopping, releasing a history whose move failed, writing the daily stamp only after a full run, the disk check (including the mount-point case), the 14-day window, the re-check under the lock, and the rule for histories recorded without a disk.

## What

Studio showed move, resize and rotate handles on an element even when it would refuse to save that edit: for example motion that comes from a helper or a loop, or an animation computed at runtime. You could drag, and only on release did Studio refuse, restore the element and show a toast. The handles promised an edit that snapped back.

Now, after an element is selected, Studio runs the same checks its saves run and hides the handles for each edit it would refuse:

- **Per handle.** Move, resize and rotate are checked separately, so an element whose loop only moves it sideways keeps its resize and rotate handles.
- **Hidden while checking.** Until the first check for an element's file finishes, its handles stay hidden. A file with no animations answers after one read, and later selections in that file are immediate. After a save, the last answer keeps the handles steady while the new one loads.
- **A press says why.** A primary-button press on an element that cannot move shows the reason at once, instead of after a drag and a snap-back. Presses stay silent while the check is still running, and a right-click never toasts.
- **One rule.** The checks are the same code the saves call: the move rule, the rotation rule and the resize rule each run before their save, so the handles and the save cannot disagree. The Design panel's position, size and rotation fields follow the same answer.
- **Groups.** A group move stays refused when a member's position comes only from a tween it shares with other members by class, as before; a single selection of that member is editable.

For resize and rotate, the refusal now happens before any write, instead of after a property split had already been written.

If the animation parse cannot be read (server or network down), an element keeps its last answer until the next selection or edit reads again.

