### Summary

The normal HDR layered compositor can produce a layer plan such as:

```text
DOM → HDR video → DOM
```

Both DOM groups are currently rasterized independently. When the first paintable layer above the leading DOM group is an opaque native HDR video that covers the complete output canvas, the lower DOM raster cannot contribute a pixel to the final frame.

Avoiding that capture removes one full-page screenshot, PNG decode, and RGBA-to-RGB48 blit per eligible frame.

### Affected version

Observed in HyperFrames v0.8.114 (`01c6f6004a00ecfa6e77ab4025b47a46a5e925fc`). Please confirm the current compositor topology on `main`.

### Safety requirements

Eligibility should be conservative and based on existing stacking and geometry state:

- the first composite layer is DOM;
- the first paintable layer above it is a native HDR video;
- opacity is effectively 1 and the video is active;
- x/y are the frame origin and paint/layout dimensions cover the full output;
- the source frame dimensions and raw-frame size are compatible with a full-frame blit;
- clip is absent or exactly full bounds;
- border radius is zero;
- transform is absent or identity.

HDR images, transitions, `elementFilter` captures, SDR capture, partial clips, non-identity transforms, and ambiguous geometry should remain on the legacy path.

### Fail-safe behavior

Geometry alone is insufficient. The lower DOM capture should be deferred until the eligible HDR frame has actually been read and blitted successfully.

The HDR video blit can expose a small typed result such as `blitted | skipped | failed`. Only `blitted` permits dropping the deferred DOM layer. On `skipped`, `failed`, incomplete reads, or exceptions, clear the uncommitted canvas and replay that frame once through the existing path with the optimization disabled.

### Validation

Regression tests should cover:

- successful DOM → opaque full-frame HDR video → DOM;
- opacity below the opaque threshold;
- smaller dimensions or non-zero x/y;
- border radius, non-identity transform, and partial clip;
- an exact full-bounds clip;
- HDR read/blit failure and one-pass legacy fallback;
- transition/`elementFilter` exclusion;
- preservation of the upper DOM layer.

Output should be pixel-identical to the legacy compositor for eligible and fallback cases.

### Summary

After native HDR video is composited, an upper DOM overlay may remain visually identical across consecutive frames while the HDR background continues to change. The HDR layered path still performs a transparent full-page screenshot and PNG decode for every occurrence.

A bounded previous-state cache can reuse the decoded transparent RGBA overlay while rebuilding the HDR canvas normally for every frame.

### Affected version

Observed in HyperFrames v0.8.114 (`01c6f6004a00ecfa6e77ab4025b47a46a5e925fc`). Please confirm whether current `main` has an equivalent DOM-raster cache.

### Proposed scope

V1 should cache only:

- the immediately previous strict state key;
- one decoded RGBA upper-DOM buffer;
- dimensions and minimal validation metadata.

It should not cache the RGB48 canvas, HDR video, final composite, encoded frame, audio, lower DOM, or a history of states.

On a hit, build the current HDR canvas as usual and blend the cached RGBA overlay with the existing `blitRgba8OverRgb48le()` path.

### Strict pre-capture key

Reuse must be decided before `Page.captureScreenshot()`. The key should include the existing layer/stacking state and all raster-affecting state for the shown DOM subtree:

- element and z-order identity;
- visibility, opacity, geometry, transform, clip, and border radius;
- text content and text-node rectangles for line wrapping;
- attributes and raster-affecting computed styles;
- pseudo-element content and styles;
- image identity and readiness;
- font readiness;
- viewport and device-pixel state.

Unknown dynamic pixel sources should fail closed to a miss. Examples include canvas, SVG, video, running animations, custom elements/shadow DOM, CSS Paint API output, unloaded assets, and animated or unverified image formats.

### Lifecycle and invalidation

- one entry per page/render session;
- exact key equality only;
- miss invalidates the previous entry;
- capture/decode/blit failure never promotes stale state;
- dimension or buffer-size mismatch misses;
- transitions and `elementFilter` captures stay disabled initially;
- no persistent disk cache.

### Observability

Add counters for hits, misses, actual screenshots, peak cache bytes, state-key time, and cached-blit time. `domLayerCaptures` should continue to mean real screenshots.

### Validation

Tests should cover text/opacity/transform/src/z-order invalidation, unknown dynamic elements, key failures, stale-cache prevention after decode failure, transition exclusion, and the key correctness case: the HDR background changes while an identical upper raster is reused.

Acceptance should require exact decoded-frame equivalence to the uncached path and identical decoded audio. The cache should remain bounded to one RGBA frame.

### Describe the bug

In the HyperFrames desktop app, a project whose `index.html` has a CSS `url(&quot;assets/image.png&quot;)` inside a `style` attribute makes the main process spin at 100% CPU from launch, climb past 1 GB and crash with `EXC_BREAKPOINT` (V8 out of memory) about 90 seconds later. Home builds that project's preview at launch, so the app crash-loops on every relaunch until the file is edited by hand.

Cause, confirmed with the inspector attached to the main process: by the time `recordPreviewReferences` (`packages/studio-server/src/helpers/previewReads.ts`) scans the preview HTML, the image has been inlined, so the unquoted `url(` branch of `REFERENCE` captures `&quot;data:image/png;base64,iVBOR...&quot;`. The leading `&quot;` defeats the scheme check (`/^(?:[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:|[/#])/i`), so the base64 is treated as a relative file path. `linksOnTheWay` then walks it one "folder" per `/` (base64 is full of them) and `recordPreviewRead` adds every ancestor of that path to the reads set. The cost is quadratic in the image size: for an 877 KB PNG the "path" was 1,169,082 characters with 20,198 slashes.

Where the `&quot;` comes from: the desktop app's bundled takeover-explainer format (`guides/formats/takeover-explainer/skills/takeover-explainer/scripts/build.py`) writes `background-image:url(&quot;...&quot;)` in five places, so any takeover-style film with a large enough image hits this. That script is not in this repo, so I'm flagging it here.

### Minimal reproduction

A verbatim copy of `linksOnTheWay` and `recordPreviewReferences` from `previewReads.ts`, run on a one-element page in its post-inlining form, against the same page quoted with `'`. Node built-ins only.

```js
// repro.mjs
import { readlinkSync, mkdtempSync } from "node:fs";
import { dirname, join, parse, resolve } from "node:path";
import { randomBytes } from "node:crypto";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
const REFERENCE = /\b(?:src|href|poster|data-composition-src)\s*=\s*(?:"([^"\n]*)"|'([^'\n]*)')|url\(\s*(?:"([^"\n]*)"|'([^'\n]*)'|([^"'\s)]+))/gi;
const readLink = (p) => { try { return readlinkSync(p); } catch { return null; } };
function linksOnTheWay(path) {
  const passed = []; let at = parse(path).root; let rest = path.slice(at.length).split(/[\\/]+/);
  for (let hops = 0; rest.length > 0; ) {
    const part = rest.shift(); if (!part || part === ".") continue;
    if (part === "..") { at = dirname(at); continue; }
    const next = join(at, part); const target = hops < 40 ? readLink(next) : null;
    if (target === null) { at = next; continue; }
    hops++; passed.push(next); const root = parse(target).root; if (root) at = root;
    rest = [...target.slice(root.length).split(/[\\/]+/), ...rest];
  }
  passed.push(at); return passed;
}
function recordPreviewReferences(projectDir, html, reads) {
  const named = new Set();
  for (const m of html.matchAll(REFERENCE)) {
    const url = (m[1] ?? m[2] ?? m[3] ?? m[4] ?? m[5] ?? "").trim();
    if (!url || /^(?:[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:|[/#])/i.test(url)) continue;
    named.add(url.split(/[?#]/)[0]);
  }
  for (const p of named) {
    const read = join(projectDir, p);
    for (const found of [read, ...linksOnTheWay(read)])
      for (let q = resolve(found).toLowerCase(); !reads.has(q); q = dirname(q)) reads.add(q);
  }
  return named;
}
const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "hf-repro-"));
for (const kb of [16, 32, 64, 128]) {
  const b64 = randomBytes(kb * 1024).toString("base64");
  for (const [label, attr] of [["&quot; (bug)", `url(&quot;data:image/png;base64,${b64}&quot;)`], ["' (control)", `url('data:image/png;base64,${b64}')`]]) {
    const reads = new Set(); const t = performance.now();
    const named = recordPreviewReferences(dir, `<div style="background-image:${attr}"></div>`, reads);
    const chars = [...reads].reduce((n, s) => n + s.length, 0);
    console.log(`${kb}KB ${label} refs=${named.size} time=${(performance.now() - t).toFixed(0)}ms reads=${reads.size} storedChars=${(chars / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M`);
  }
}
