### Additional context

Possible fixes, any one of which would have prevented this:

- Decode HTML entities (at least `&quot;`, `&#39;`, `&amp;`) in the captured value before the scheme check. The browser does this for attributes, so `url(&quot;X&quot;)` really means `url("X")`.
- Skip any captured reference longer than a sane path length (say 4096 characters) before `linksOnTheWay`.
- In takeover-explainer's `build.py`, write `url('...')` instead of `url(&quot;...&quot;)`.
- On the desktop side, Home building a crashing preview at launch turns one bad file into a crash loop. Something that lets the person reach the project would make it recoverable without a terminal.

Workaround for anyone hitting this: with the app quit, replace `url(&quot;X&quot;)` with `url('X')` in the project's `index.html` (`grep -rl --include='*.html' 'url(&quot;' ~/.hyperframes-studio/` finds it).

### Describe the bug

With the default drawElement capture (macOS, hardware GPU), a sub-composition mounted with `data-composition-src` whose host slot has `data-start` greater than 0 is missing from every frame of a render long enough to take the parallel drawElement streaming path (≥ 700 frames). The same composition renders the block correctly with `PRODUCER_EXPERIMENTAL_FAST_CAPTURE=false` (screenshot capture), in `hyperframes snapshot`, and in short renders. `hyperframes check` passes. Nothing warns, so the output silently loses the graphic.

I first reported this to HeyGen support on 2026-10-09. Support reproduced that screenshot capture renders the block and asked me to file it here with a `--debug` log.

### Minimal reproduction

```bash
ffmpeg -f lavfi -i testsrc2=size=1920x1080:rate=30 -f lavfi -i sine=f=440:sample_rate=48000 \
  -t 100 -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -g 30 -c:a aac clip.mp4
npx hyperframes init repro --non-interactive --video clip.mp4 --skip-transcribe
cd repro
npx hyperframes add lt-side-rule --no-clipboard
```

In `index.html`, replace the `<!-- Add your clips here ... -->` comment with this one line. Everything else is `init`'s default, and `compositions/lt-side-rule.html` is unmodified from `hyperframes add`:

```html
<div id="title" data-composition-id="lt-side-rule" data-composition-src="compositions/lt-side-rule.html" data-start="0.3" data-duration="4.8" data-track-index="1" data-width="1920" data-height="1080"></div>
```

### Steps to reproduce

1. `npx hyperframes render --quality draft -o default.mp4`
2. `PRODUCER_EXPERIMENTAL_FAST_CAPTURE=false npx hyperframes render --quality draft -o screenshot.mp4`
3. Grab a frame inside the block's window from each: `ffmpeg -ss 2 -i default.mp4 -frames:v 1 default.png` (and the same for `screenshot.mp4`).

### Expected behavior

The lower third ("DR. MAYA CHEN / Host · Neuroscientist", bottom left) is visible from 0.3 s to 5.1 s in both renders, as it is in `npx hyperframes snapshot --at 2`.

### Actual behavior

- `default.mp4`: the lower third is absent from every frame. Summary line:
  `drawelement capture · hardware gpu · compile 0.5s · extract 0.0s · audio 1.0s · probe 0.0s · setup 0.2s · capture 19.7s · encode (during capture) 19.7s · assemble 0.5s`
  The log picks `Fast capture: verified parallel drawElement streaming preferred over single-worker inversion (3000 frames >= 700; ...)`.
- `screenshot.mp4`: the lower third is present (`screenshot capture · hardware gpu · ...`).
- Changing only `data-start="0.3"` to `data-start="0"` makes the block render with the default capture.
- A 6-second version of the same composition (under 700 frames) renders the block with the default capture.
- `HF_DE_PARALLEL_ROUTER=false` (one worker) still drops it; as support pointed out, that switch only disables the parallel workers, not drawElement.
- With `--debug`, `compiled/index.html` in the debug folder still contains the slot (`data-start="0.3"`) and the inlined block, so the block seems to be lost during capture, not compilation. The debug `render.log` (below) never mentions the sub-composition.

Same result on 0.8.143 and 0.8.145. First seen on a 4K, 97.7 s project rendered with `--quality delivery`.

