### Problem

HyperFrames 0.8.158 (the same since 0.8.153), Windows 11, Chrome Headless Shell 152.0.7977.30.

`CheckDeadline` stops a check once its browser has been ready for `CHECK_DEADLINE_MS = 120_000` and reports `check_runtime_failure: Check did not finish within its deadline.` The timer covers the whole audit, and no option or environment variable changes it. Measured on 0.8.158; the code is unchanged in v0.8.162 ([packages/cli/src/utils/checkDeadline.ts, line 6](https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes/blob/v0.8.162/packages/cli/src/utils/checkDeadline.ts#L6), the timer at [lines 39-47](https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes/blob/v0.8.162/packages/cli/src/utils/checkDeadline.ts#L39-L47), started by `onBrowserReady`).

**What it breaks**

Our compositions are architecture explainers of one to four minutes. `check --json --at-transitions` of a 65 s one samples about 1,600 moments (every tween boundary and the midpoint of each two in a row). Under 0.8.106 that check took 12 to 18 minutes in one pass on this machine (714 to 1,103 s, depending on load). Under 0.8.153 and later every such check stops at the deadline, so the full audit cannot pass on any of them. A check of some 200 `--at` moments also passes 120 s when the machine is busy.

**Why `--range` does not help on its own**

A check judges its findings over its own samples (file references at v0.8.162):

- a layout issue seen at one sample is `info`, and a `content_overlap` is an error only when held 500 ms (`applyPersistenceTier`, `packages/cli/src/utils/layoutAudit.ts` line 309);
- repeats collapse into one finding (`collapseStaticLayoutIssues`, `layoutAudit.ts` line 223);
- contrast is audited at five moments picked from the check's samples (`selectContrastTimes`, `packages/cli/src/utils/checkPipeline.ts` line 105), and a failure is an error only when held at two of them (`contrastFailureHeld`, `checkPipeline.ts` line 1444);
- the overlap sweep runs at 8 fps over the range (`OVERLAP_SAMPLE_FPS`, `checkPipeline.ts` line 491).

So the windows of a long composition, each checked alone, report different findings and severities from one check of the whole: an overlap held across a window's edge can fall to `info` in both windows, and a contrast failure seen once in the whole check can be held in a window.

A minimal reproduction is under Additional context.

### Proposed solution

Either one would let us drop the copy described under Alternatives considered:

1. **A configurable deadline**: an option (`--deadline <ms>`) or variable (`HYPERFRAMES_CHECK_DEADLINE_MS`), or a deadline per sample, so a stuck script is still caught at the sample it sticks on and a long audit finishes.
2. **A check in parts that the CLI merges itself**: for example `check --emit-raw` writing a part's raw issues, contrast entries and layout signatures, and `check merge <part>...` applying the persistence tier, the collapse, the contrast selection and held test, and the frozen-sweep test over all the parts' samples, so the parts of a long composition report exactly what one check would.

### Alternatives considered

We stay on 0.8.152, the last release without the deadline.

We also built a check in parts for 0.8.158 and keep it on a branch: every part run with `--no-collapse-static --max-issues 1000000 --no-contrast`, contrast run once at the five moments one check would pick, and the raw issues merged with a copy of the functions above, held to the installed package by a test. On our 65 s composition the parts report what one check of 0.8.106 reported, sample for sample, but the full check takes 1,117 s against 714 s in one pass, and on a 101 s one 1,123 s against 665 s (each part pays its own browser start-up and lint; see also #5681 on lint's speed). It is a copy of your rules that would have to be redone at every release, and it cannot reproduce the frozen-sweep test or the rotation detectors across parts, so we have not shipped it.

Capping the samples with `--max-transition-samples` keeps a check under the deadline, but it drops the transition samples the audit is for.

