**Why not only the retry.** The other fix is to catch `DrawElementCaptureError` and `DrawElementVerificationError` around `runCaptureSegmentedStage`, and apply `replanAfterFailure(capturePlan, { kind: "draw_element_capture" })`, as the streaming route does. That should happen as well, for frames that really are dropped. On its own, though, it would re-render every segment in screenshots because of one valid flat frame, and the output would no longer match the one pass. drawElement and screenshot frames differ: the self-verify above measures 33.4 dB between them. Also, `computeSegmentPlanHash` does not include the capture mode, so a `--resume` after such a fallback could join drawElement segments to screenshot segments in one video.

A reproducible deferred-DOM capture case on `hyperframes@0.8.145` (npm gitHead `76f8a567a5f4ff7b5b75b0c67c770b7152bf56b6`). Related to #4435, but this is a GSAP + transparent PNG-sequence case and shows pending virtual-rAF work, rather than a duck-typed timeline/ProRes case.

The normal composition is clean. A composition that defers a DOM update through requestAnimationFrame renders successfully but drops visible text/image content from initial frames of each worker. Snapshot/browser checks look correct once deferred updates have settled.

A minimal way to expose the readiness boundary is an onUpdate callback that schedules a reveal through several rAF turns:

```html
<!doctype html><html><head>
<script src="./node_modules/gsap/dist/gsap.min.js"></script>
<style>html,body{margin:0;background:transparent}#text{color:white;font:48px sans-serif}</style>
</head><body>
<div data-composition-id="main" data-start="0" data-duration="2" data-width="640" data-height="360">
  <div id="text">A complete headline</div>
</div>
<script>
const text = document.getElementById('text');
const tl = gsap.timeline({paused:true,onUpdate(){
  text.style.visibility='hidden';
  let pending=10;
  function reveal(){
    if(--pending>0) requestAnimationFrame(reveal);
    else text.style.visibility='visible';
  }
  requestAnimationFrame(reveal);
}});
tl.to('#text',{x:4,duration:2,ease:'none'},0);
window.__timelines={main:tl};
tl.seek(0);
</script></body></html>
```

Install `hyperframes@0.8.145` and `gsap@3.15.0`, then render with `PRODUCER_FORCE_SCREENSHOT=true`:

```sh
hyperframes render --format png-sequence --output frames --fps 24 --workers 2 --browser-gpu --experimental-fast-capture=false
```

Measured with a fuller 48-frame regression fixture containing multiple text regions, an image card, and a locally bundled web font:

- Windows x64, Chrome Headless Shell 152.0.7977.30, Microsoft Basic Render Driver: **18/48 frames** have missing content with two workers; **9/48** with one worker.
- Linux x64, same Chrome, AMD hardware WebGL renderer: **18/48** with two workers.
- Explicit software capture has the same deferred-work result.
- After draining finite deferred author callbacks at the current virtual time, waiting for fonts/image decode and two native paint frames: **0/48** missing frames with two workers on both platforms. Ordinary compositions and a deterministic timestamp-based continuous canvas loop also remain clean.

This is an adversarial readiness regression, not a claim that every real-world GPU missing-layer report has this cause. It makes the missing per-seek readiness boundary observable without depending on a random GPU failure.

In the published [frame capture implementation](https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes/blob/76f8a567a5f4ff7b5b75b0c67c770b7152bf56b6/packages/engine/src/services/frameCapture.ts), font/image readiness happens during initialization, while subsequent capture can follow the seek without a general native paint barrier. Also, window.requestAnimationFrame is virtualized: awaiting two ordinary rAF callbacks from the capture barrier can stall unless virtual callbacks are explicitly driven. Native compositor waits need the stored originalRequestAnimationFrame (or an equivalent explicit compositor commit).

Could the engine define and enforce a per-frame readiness contract here? A useful fix would settle finite deferred DOM work at the authored time, check newly active assets, commit paint before capture, and recapture/report suspected incomplete frames. Continuous deterministic rAF loops should remain supported. Unconditionally double-capturing every frame adds substantial cost, so confirmation can be reserved for startup or instability signals.

## Describe the bug

`hyperframes add <tag> --json` returns `ok: true` and exits 0 even when every block download fails. It also reports success when only some blocks install. Scripts and coding agents therefore cannot use either the JSON status or exit code to detect an incomplete installation, and JSON mode drops the individual failure reasons.

## Minimal reproduction

From a built repository checkout, save this as `repro-tag.mjs` in the repository root:

```js
import "./packages/cli/scripts/test-home.mjs";
import { mkdtempSync, writeFileSync, rmSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";

process.env.HYPERFRAMES_NO_TELEMETRY = "1";
const project = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "hf-tag-repro-"));
process.once("exit", () => rmSync(project, { recursive: true, force: true }));
writeFileSync(join(project, "hyperframes.json"), JSON.stringify({
  registry: "https://tag-repro.example.com/registry",
  paths: { blocks: "compositions", components: "components", assets: "assets" },
}));

// Valid registry metadata, followed by a missing HTML payload.
globalThis.fetch = async (input) => {
  const path = new URL(input).pathname;
  if (path.endsWith("/registry.json")) return Response.json({
    name: "repro", homepage: "https://example.com",
    items: [{ name: "broken", type: "hyperframes:block" }],
  });
  if (path.endsWith("/blocks/broken/registry-item.json")) return Response.json({
    name: "broken", type: "hyperframes:block", title: "Broken", description: "Repro",
    tags: ["sample-tag"], dimensions: { width: 1920, height: 1080 }, duration: 3,
    files: [{ path: "broken.html", target: "compositions/broken.html", type: "hyperframes:composition" }],
  });
  return new Response("not found", { status: 404 });
};

process.argv = ["bun", "hyperframes", "add", "sample-tag", "--dir", project, "--json", "--no-clipboard"];
await import("./packages/cli/src/cli.ts");
```

The fixture supplies valid registry metadata and an HTTP 404 for the block's HTML. Only HTTP responses are synthetic: CLI dispatch, tag resolution, downloading, filesystem writes and process exit handling are the real implementation. The repository's test-home helper and a temporary project keep cache/config/project writes isolated. No network or clipboard access is needed.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Use main at `10c724bf555f8355199bd89db1a02fc159909dc9` (version 0.8.186) with the repository dependencies and packages built.
2. Run `bun repro-tag.mjs` and inspect the process exit status.

## Expected behavior

The command exits nonzero and reports `ok: false`, identifying the failed block and the HTTP 404 cause. When some blocks install, it retains those successful files and still reports the failures.

## Actual behavior

Exit code: **0**. No block HTML is installed. Stdout:

```json
{"ok":true,"tag":"sample-tag","installed":[],"warnings":[]}
```

With one failed and one successful block, the process still exits 0 with `ok: true`; only the successful item appears in `installed`.

## Environment

- Hyperframes CLI 0.8.186; current-main base `10c724bf555f8355199bd89db1a02fc159909dc9`.
- Linux x86_64, Node 26.11.1, Bun 1.4.1, Citty 0.2.2. The repository pins Bun 1.4.4; the reproduction and tests used the already-installed 1.4.1.
- Reproduced through `packages/cli/src/cli.ts`, including its real process exit boundary.
- Full workspace build/typecheck is limited by missing installed inspector/AWS dependencies; focused command/installer/resolver tests run successfully. No packages were installed for this investigation.

## Additional context

The tag loop catches each installation error, suppresses it in JSON mode, then calls `tagAddJson`, which always returns `ok: true`. No failure reaches the root command boundary.

#4432 preserves warnings from successful installs; #648 and open #5256 concern manifest refresh during discovery. This failure happens after discovery, while installing files. I have a focused fix prepared that preserves successful installs and reports each failure.
